******************* Checkfile **********************Copyright (c) 1992, by John R. WainwrightPermission is granted for any non-commercial use.Checkfile is a quickie checkbook program that grew.  It started as aQuickBasic program I wrote for a friend to use on a TANDY 1000. I figured this would be a good way to learn to use the TERMCAP file in a"C" program.  So far, it seems to work pretty well on terminals, I havetested it on a TANDY 2000 and a nameless "XT-Clone" via null modem, andover the phone on a Tandy 1000.  The only problem I had was with the"clear to end of screen" function - maybe a bad entry in my "ansi"section in TERMCAP. The Checkfile Program is real basic - no frills.  You enter the data, itstores it, lets you edit it, adds up the amounts.  If you want it tokeep a balance, you MUST enter all checks and other debits as negativenumbers - at this point it treats all entries the same.  (Doesn't know acheck from a deposit, it just adds). The TERMCAP usage is taken right out of the OS9/68000 manuals. The program starts out using a file called "testfile.dat" in the currentdata directory.  You have the option of changing to another data fileafter the program starts.  If it doesn't find a data file, it willcreate it the first time you add a record.  A sample data file isincluded. The add record routine uses a form that I have used in a lot of littleprograms with data entry.  You are prompted for each field, and at theend, you are given a chance to change any field or accept the record asshown.  Note that the record number is assigned by the program - youcan't change that.  The date format is optional - I put it in that wayto make sorting easier for future enhancements."Report" is just a screen list with balance."Print Balance"  (Report without the list)The "View/Edit" selection brings up another menu.  You can move throughthe file and edit records.  "Search" will move to any selected recordnr.There is NO delete record function - you can make it ignore an entry inthe amount field by starting it with any non-numeric character.  (a "$"for example).  (You should NOT use a "$", or any other character otherthan 0-9,+,.  or - in the amount field if you want it to recoginize theentry as a number.)A more complete version (if I get around to doing it) will pay attentionto the entries in the TYPE and ACCT fields so it will "know" credits anddebits.  It will also do a report sorted by account and date (withsubtotals).  There will also probably be a "config" file with thecurrent data filename, account list, and whatever else I think of. There's lots of room for additions/improvements - maybe even drop theTERMCAPs and build it into the SKEL program that Mark Griffith posted. The "C" source is included for those who would like to play with it.  Iam still a "kindergarten level" C programmer, so I am sure that moreexperienced "C" types will see better ways to do it.  Comments andcriticism welcome. John R. WainwrightDelphi - JOHNREED    CIS - 72517,676  Genie J.Wainwright